Each brief is self-contained with verification checklists. When working on one, read the entire brief first, then execute step-by-step.

---

## Apple App Store Review Guidelines you must respect

- **§1.2 (User-Generated Content / Social):** Requires block, report, contact mechanisms. → task 07
- **§3.1.2 (Subscriptions):** Paywall must show Privacy + Terms links + auto-renewal disclosure + restore. → task 05
- **§4.8 (Sign in with Apple):** If any third-party login exists (Google, Facebook), Sign in with Apple must be at least equally prominent. → task 06
- **§5.1.1(v) (Account Deletion):** App must allow in-app account deletion (not just web). Already implemented via `delete-account` Edge Function. Verify UI accessible from Settings.
- **§5.1.2(i) (Third-Party AI):** App must disclose use of third-party AI (OpenAI). Add to Privacy Policy. → task 13

---

## Codebase conventions

**iOS (Swift):**
- Use `@Observable` macro for view models (we're iOS 17+)
- Async/await for all networking; no callbacks
- All Supabase calls go through `SupabaseService.shared` (don't call `supabase-swift` SDK directly from views)
- All AI calls go through Supabase Edge Functions (NEVER hit OpenAI from iOS — see task 01)
- Premium gating queries the `subscriptions` table (server source of truth — see task 09)

**Web (TypeScript/React):**
- Functional components only; no class components
- Use shadcn/ui primitives where possible
- Use Tailwind utilities; minimal custom CSS
- Routing via React Router or whichever the project uses (don't introduce a second router)
- Custom hooks in `src/hooks/`
- API/SDK wrappers in `src/lib/`
- All analytics events go through `src/lib/analytics.ts` (typed wrapper around Plausible)

**Backend (Supabase):**
- All migrations in `supabase/migrations/` — timestamped, idempotent where possible
- All Edge Functions in `supabase/functions/<name>/index.ts` with shared `cors.ts` pattern
- RLS enabled on EVERY table in `public` schema; no exceptions
- Edge Functions verify Supabase JWT via `getUser(token)` before doing anything user-specific
- The webhook `apple-notifications-v2` is the ONE exception — deployed with `--no-verify-jwt` because Apple sends its own JWS
- Tighten CORS: prefer specific origin (`https://yourdategenie.com`) over `*` for any function called from web. iOS doesn't need CORS.

---

## Files you should NEVER touch without explicit permission

- `ios/Secrets.xcconfig` — local secrets, git-ignored. Anjela manages this.
- `supabase/migrations/<existing>` — never edit migrations after they've shipped. Always add a new migration on top.
- `legal/terms-of-service-draft.md` — entity already updated to LLC; further legal edits require lawyer review.
- `.env.local` — local secrets, git-ignored.
- `.env.production` — production secrets, managed in Vercel/Netlify dashboard.
- Anything under `node_modules/`, `.next/`, `dist/`, `build/`, `Pods/`, `DerivedData/`.

---

## Files where you should be especially careful

- `ios/Info.plist` — has Apple-mandated keys. Don't remove existing privacy strings (`NSCalendarsUsageDescription`, `NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription`, `NSPhotoLibraryAddUsageDescription`). Adding new keys requires audit.
- `ios/YourDateGenie/Config.swift` — ALL secrets are read from `Secrets.xcconfig` via Info.plist substitution. NEVER add a hardcoded fallback string for any key.
- `supabase/functions/*/index.ts` — these run on every API call. Don't introduce blocking sync I/O. Always handle CORS preflight.
- `src/lib/firebase.ts` — only used for waitlist. Don't extend this for app data; that's Supabase's job.

---

## Patterns we DO use

- iOS: Result-type returns from async services; views use `do { try await … } catch { … }` with user-friendly error messages
- iOS: Pull-to-refresh on lists; loading skeletons; error toasts via `ToastManager`
- Web: Form validation with native HTML5 + small inline error messages (no big form libraries)
- Web: Optimistic UI updates where safe (waitlist confirmation appears immediately, then rolls back if Firestore rejects)
- Backend: Soft-delete patterns (set `deleted_at` instead of DELETE) where audit matters; hard-delete for `delete-account` to satisfy GDPR

## Patterns we DON'T use

- iOS: No SwiftUI Combine publishers in new code (use async/await)
- iOS: No third-party DI containers (Swinject, etc.) — keep `.shared` singletons for now
- Web: No Redux / Zustand for now (state is component-local or React Query)
- Web: No CSS-in-JS libraries (Tailwind only)
- Backend: No raw `psql` migrations — use the Supabase CLI migration tooling

---

## Marketing & growth strategy (informs build decisions)

- **Channel strategy: organic-first, community-led.** Reddit, IG, TikTok, in-person events. NOT paid Meta/TikTok ads at launch (founder decision).
- **Implication for iOS:** We do NOT need `NSUserTrackingUsageDescription` in Info.plist for v1. Reddit traffic doesn't require IDFA. If we ever turn on Meta/TikTok pixel attribution post-launch, we'll add the permission string in a v1.1 update.
- **Implication for web:** Plausible is enough; no GA4, no Facebook Pixel, no LinkedIn Insight Tag.

---

## Communication style

When you finish a task, output a single line summary in the chat: what changed, what files, what remaining steps require Anjela (manual Supabase/App Store/Apple Developer console actions). Don't over-explain — Anjela reads the diff.

When you find a problem mid-task, stop and ask. Don't silently expand scope. The task briefs are scoped intentionally; bigger changes need a refreshed brief.

When you're unsure of a project convention, prefer reading existing similar files in the same directory over inventing a new pattern.
